     "notes": {
      "text": "The opening is a race to develop and control the centre. 1.d4 frees your queen's bishop, stops ...e5 and controls c5. Unlike 1.e4, the pawn is already guarded by the queen, so games tend to be slower and more closed, which suits the steady London set-up. 1.e4 is the more popular, sharper choice.",
      "credit": {
       "source": "Wikibooks, Chess Opening Theory",
       "url": "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Chess_Opening_Theory",
       "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
       "licenceUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
       "authors": "Wikibooks contributors (see the page history)"
      }
     }
